{"title":"1983 IMO Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_5\">Problem 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Is it possible to choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/2/7f285f05f4d501652175d1befe9189270b28e1ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1983$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> distinct positive integers, all less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/d/e7d8b576968ccdbfeff38053b7c606905adb945e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10^5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"15\" />, no three of which are consecutive terms of an arithmetic progression? Justify your answer.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The answer is yes. We will construct a sequence of integers that satisfies the requirements.\n</p><p>Take the following definition of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" /> in base 3 has the same digits as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> in base 2. For example, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/b/b8b66e4be3258b774c6dcdbd0fbddfc3e644664b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6=110_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/6/a061fb07af219fb6b599cc60f58ded44e1974cff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_6=110_3=12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"120\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>First, we will prove that no three <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" />'s are in an arithmetic progression. Assume for sake of contradiction, that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99b558ab142d3e1b06b16d99a645dda16d81c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i &lt; j &lt; k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"73\" height=\"16\" /> are numbers such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/e/4ae245a101985b5576f18f59f48c24aef70c8ea1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2a_j=a_i + a_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"104\" height=\"16\" />. Consider the base 3 representations of both sides of the equation. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/8/0785f19824c8b23ab3dfe000b471994078fdf589.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"15\" height=\"12\" /> consists of just 0's and 1's in base three, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e89e116b42dc1245f45cb8fb35611c5b082f2c3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2a_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"16\" /> consists of just 0's and 2's base 3. No whenever <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e89e116b42dc1245f45cb8fb35611c5b082f2c3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2a_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"16\" /> has a 0, both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/a/bea4d576937a04ec200b1037fc8ee33e8f744cb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> must have a 0, since both could only have a 0 or 1 in that place. Similarly, whenever <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e89e116b42dc1245f45cb8fb35611c5b082f2c3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2a_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"16\" /> has a 2, both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/a/bea4d576937a04ec200b1037fc8ee33e8f744cb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> must have a 1 in that place. This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/0/b3028eb1bbdf90e6c4a93121f4dffd9783aecacd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i=a_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"56\" height=\"10\" />, which is a contradiction.\n</p><p>Now since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/6/b4697bf49a9a471a7cdb77e4fa15181f25e4cb81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1983=11110111111_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"166\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/4/3b4359af20ec50963b70fae3bb0c8e1b1e471176.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{1983} = 11110111111_3 &lt; \\frac{3^{11}}{2} &lt; 10^5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"268\" height=\"39\" />. Hence, the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/f/00f22ae7bda675bdf264ae4efa1d043148db5abe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{a_1,a_2,...a_{1983}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"119\" height=\"18\" /> is a set of 1983 integers less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/d/e7d8b576968ccdbfeff38053b7c606905adb945e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10^5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"15\" /> with no three in arithmetic progression.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO\" title=\"1983 IMO\">1983 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1983 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1983 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1983 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1983 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1983 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1983 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1983 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1983_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1983 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and 